Add gdbserver support: the LikeOS low-level module.

gdbserver is the other half of a debugger.  It holds the ptrace end on the
machine running the program and speaks gdb's remote protocol over a socket or a
serial line, so the symbols, the source tree and the debugger itself stay
somewhere else.  On this system that matters more than it does on a desktop:
the native gdb is 11M stripped and carries 148M of its own DWARF unstripped,
while this is 550K.

Three files.

gdbserver/configure.srv gets an x86_64-*-likeos* entry, and that entry is what
turns gdbserver on at all: the TOP-LEVEL configure sources this file and drops
gdbserver from the subdirectory list when it names no case for the host.  The
object list is the smallest one that works -- the new module, gdbserver's own
fork-child, gdb's shared fork-inferior and amd64 target-description builder,
i387-fp for the FXSAVE image, and x86-low + nat/x86-dregs for the hardware
watchpoint registers.

gdbserver/Makefile.in lists the new source in SFILES.  Nothing is built from
that list -- the objects come from configure.srv -- but it is what `make tags'
and the distribution rules walk, and a file missing from it is a file that
quietly is not there.

gdbserver/likeos-low.cc is the module.  It is written against the same kernel
interface as gdb/likeos-nat.c -- LikeOS's own ptrace(2) -- and reaches the same
conclusions about it, because the kernel forces them:

  - Registers are moved as whole blocks, because that is how PTRACE_GETREGS
    reports them.  The translation table is keyed by register NAME and the
    numbers are looked up once from the target description, rather than being
    an array indexed by register number: a table in the wrong order compiles
    perfectly and reports one register's value under another's name, whereas an
    unknown name throws at start-up.

  - The target description asks for the "segments" feature, where the NetBSD
    equivalent does not.  That is %fs_base and %gs_base, and %fs_base is where
    a thread's TLS block lives -- without it a thread-local variable cannot be
    located, and on this system errno is one.

  - wait() waits with -1 and not with the process id.  The kernel matches a
    POSITIVE waitpid selector against the TASK id, so waiting for the process
    would report only the thread group leader and every stop belonging to any
    other thread would sit uncollected.

  - What comes back from wait is a TASK id, which for a thread is not the
    process id.  It is mapped to (process, thread) by asking which process owns
    it; a task that belongs to none of them is released rather than reported,
    because the forked child of a traced process is stopped along with its
    parent and leaving it parked hangs the shell that forked it.

  - The whole event decode is gated on PTRACE_SETOPTIONS having been issued.
    Clone and exit stops are raised only when their option is set, but an EXEC
    stop is raised for every exec by a traced task, because it is also the
    PTRACE_TRACEME + execve bootstrap.  Reported as TARGET_WAITKIND_EXECD it
    lands in startup_inferior, whose last status handle_status replays as the
    answer to gdb's first `?' packet -- so gdb's first event of the session is
    an exec, follow_exec sets the program space's solib ops, start_remote_1's
    post_create_inferior sets them again, and gdb dies on
    `Assertion `m_solib_ops == nullptr' failed'.  Until the options are set an
    exec stop is gdbserver's own and is reported as the plain SIGTRAP that
    startup_inferior counts.

  - Clone and thread-exit stops are consumed here rather than reported.  They
    exist so that a thread can be registered the moment it is born and dropped
    the moment it dies; passed upwards they surface as `received signal
    SIGTRAP' once per thread.

  - The event value comes back through DATA, not ADDR.  PTRACE_GETEVENTMSG is
    the one request on this system whose pointer argument is DATA, and getting
    it the other way round does not fail loudly -- the kernel finds a null
    pointer, refuses it, and the value is silently left at zero.

  - A resume is aimed at the thread that STOPPED, not at the thread group
    leader.  Every trace stop parks the whole group and any member's PT_CONT
    releases all of it, but the kernel refuses to resume a task that is not
    itself stopped -- so naming the leader fails outright once a leader has
    exited while its threads run on.

  - read_auxv is served from PTRACE_GETAUXV.  Executables here are
    position-independent and this vector is the only way to learn the bias one
    was loaded at; without it `break main' resolves to a link-time offset with
    nothing mapped at it.

  - pid_to_exec_file is served from PTRACE_GETEXECPATH, which is what makes
    --attach useful: there is no per-process filesystem to read the program's
    path out of, and neither the process name (a basename) nor argv[0] (which
    for a login shell is "-bash") names a file that can be opened.

Deliberately not implemented: fork and vfork events, which would oblige this
module to detach the parked child and which nothing here needs to follow;
syscall catching; tracepoints and the in-process agent; and
qXfer:libraries-svr4, because gdb walks the loader's link map through ordinary
memory reads when the target does not offer it.

The debugger at the other end has to know this system's OS ABI to relocate a
position-independent executable and to find shared libraries.  One built from
this port's source with --target=x86_64-unknown-likeos does -- the OS ABI name
gdbserver puts in its target description is the one patch 0002 registered -- and
a stock gdb gets most of the way with `set osabi GNU/Linux'.

